059],[137.2364309,-5.0652205],[137.2364309,-5.0651351],[137.2364309,-5.0650497],[137.2365163,-5.0649643]]},"properties":{"role":"route","length_m":4461.5,"max_slope_deg":18.36,"climb_m":222.2,"descent_m":133.5}},{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[137.2295139,-5.0934896]},"properties":{"role":"stop","order":1,"name":"Inverted Channel Ridge Foot","snapped_m":0.0,"why_habitability":"Located at the base of the sinuous ridge on walkable valley floor ground. From this position, astronauts can sample eroded talus and cemented sandstones shedding directly from the inverted riverbed. These deposits record ancient mineral precipitation from liquid water that cemented the stream sediments.","source_url":"https://www.uahirise.org/PSP_006855_1750"}},{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[137.2354916,-5.085974]},"properties":{"role":"stop","order":2,"name":"East Wall Stratified Outcrop","snapped_m":0.0,"why_habitability":"Finely layered sedimentary bedrock exposed on the lower slopes of the eastern canyon wall. These rhythmic strata record fluctuating water levels and sediment deposition in an ancient lacustrine or groundwater-fed environment. Astronauts can sample across sequential layers to reconstruct climate history.","source_url":"https://www.uahirise.org/PSP_007501_1750"}},{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[137.237541,-5.0780313]},"properties":{"role":"stop","order":3,"name":"Debris Levees Overlook","snapped_m":0.0,"why_habitability":"From this vantage point on the eastern terrace, astronauts observe the sinuous debris flow ridge and lateral levees within the canyon below. These mass movements required pore-water saturation and groundwater exfiltration to mobilize down low slopes. Orbital data confirm polyhydrated sulfate signatures in these water-transported deposits.","source_url":"https://www.hou.usra.edu/meetings/lpsc2020/pdf/2426.pdf"}},{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[137.2354916,-5.0719675]},"properties":{"role":"stop","order":4,"name":"Canyon Chasm Overlook","snapped_m":0.0,"why_habitability":"An elevated walkable terrace overlook providing clear views of the deep canyon chasm and its exhumed channel floor deposits. From here, astronauts can visually survey and photograph the inverted riverbed and tributary alcoves carved into sulfate strata. It enables comprehensive analysis of fluvial stratigraphy without entering steep, hazardous terrain.","source_url":"https://www.hou.usra.edu/meetings/lpsc2019/pdf/3196.pdf"}},{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[137.2365163,-5.0649643]},"properties":{"role":"stop","order":5,"name":"Northern Sulfate Terrace and Basin Viewpoint","snapped_m":0.0,"why_habitability":"A broad, level plateau exposure where the layered sulfate unit transitions toward the lower alluvial fan and Gale Crater floor. Astronauts can inspect wind-exhumed sulfate yardangs and evaluate paleoflow vectors as the ancient canyon emptied into the broader basin. This terminal stop provides panoramic context of the entire aqueous depositional system.","source_url":"https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Feature/15941"}}]}